{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's argument commits a category error by conflating 'induction' (an epistemic claim that past success increases the probability of future truth) with 'critical preference' (a methodological rule to act on the best-tested theory while explicitly denying it is more probable). Popper's defense relies on the distinction that preferring Theory A does not assert 'A is likely true' (which would be induction), but rather 'A is the most rational candidate for criticism and application until falsified.' The target asserts that any relevance of the past to future action constitutes induction, thereby ignoring Popper's specific solution: that the preference is justified by the logical asymmetry of falsification (we know A has survived tests B failed, so A contains more empirical content), not by an inductive inference that A will continue to survive. By failing to engage with the non-probabilistic justification of 'verisimilitude' or 'empirical content,' the target attacks a straw man of Popperian pragmatism rather than the actual philosophical position.","counterexample":["A scientist chooses a bridge design based on calculations that have survived rigorous stress testing, not because they believe the laws of physics are 'more probable' tomorrow, but because the alternative designs have already been shown to fail under those specific conditions."],"premise":"Popper distinguishes between believing a theory is probable (induction) and preferring a theory as the best basis for action because it has withstood severe tests (critical rationalism).","successor_question":"If the preference for the better-corroborated theory is not based on the expectation that it will remain true (induction), what specific logical property does 'surviving severe tests' confer that makes it the rational choice for action?","target":"45cb94fde87f19e9d1c1a721b032b320415c8cebc3a8dab30939ac21790fad03"}]}